WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.089
Hi, welcome back to Nagarjuna.

00:00:02.089 --> 00:00:03.380
Arya Nagarjuna.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.600
We're moving on to Chapter 11.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.833
Look, the chapter 10 was tough.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.310
That fire and firewood thing is so tough.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.610
Does the fire make the firewood

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.700
or does the firewood make the fire?

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.740
And I'm happy to say that we're moving on

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.773
to a chapter where the
first verse is easier.

00:00:23.186 --> 00:00:25.394
MA YIN DGAG

00:00:25.394 --> 00:00:26.227
[everyone laughs]

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.855
So this chapter is called

00:00:28.855 --> 00:00:30.503
SNGON DANG PHYI MA'I MTHA' BRTAG PA

00:00:30.503 --> 00:00:32.270
BRTAG PA in Tibetan means

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:35.610
"pariksha" means "an
investigation", right?

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.730
And then SNGON DANG PHYI MA'I MTHA' means:

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.800
is there a front end on sansara?

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.880
And is there a back end on sansara?

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.693
And that's a very intriguing question.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.097
I called the chapter name,

00:00:51.097 --> 00:00:54.220
"An Investigation of a First Beginning,

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.970
or a Final Ending, to Things".

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.930
Is there a first beginning?

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.550
And is there a final ending to things?

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.780
So we're going to talk about that.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.253
I think if you're bored and
you want to go deeper...

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:13.780
I think it's very
interesting to try to see

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.730
why he went in this order.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.660
It seems like he's just
throwing stuff out.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.643
Almost without logic.

00:01:20.643 --> 00:01:21.935
But I think there's a deeper logic.

00:01:21.935 --> 00:01:26.080
If you could understand why
the 27 go in the order they do,

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.490
maybe you could understand
the Nargarjuna's mind somehow.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:31.500
And again, Choney Lama,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.000
as you remember from our first session,

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.608
way back two years ago,

00:01:36.608 --> 00:01:38.580
he just mixes it all up.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:43.240
He says, "The real order
is like Chapter 18 first,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.830
and then..."

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.313
Here's the first verse in Chapter 11:

00:01:50.762 --> 00:01:52.750
,SNGON MTHA' MNGON NAM ZHES ZHUS TSE,

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.150
Let's just talk about that first line.

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.303
It's very beautiful.

00:01:57.303 --> 00:01:59.540
SNGON MTHA' means a first beginning

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:00.600
to sansara,

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.830
a first beginning to our
time in this universe.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:09.830
Was there a point where
you and me first came

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.820
into this universe?

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.550
Was there a beginning
point of Michael, or Pachi,

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.958
or Tim, or Rebecca, or Pau?

00:02:17.958 --> 00:02:19.441
Was there a first...

00:02:19.441 --> 00:02:20.713
Was there a beginning point?

00:02:22.578 --> 00:02:25.990
ZHUS TSE means "when they asked him."

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:27.863
When they asked him. Who?

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:30.890
They asked Buddha.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.581
When his disciples asked Buddha.

00:02:33.581 --> 00:02:36.939
ZHES - "quote mark."

00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:39.070
"Is there a first beginning to things?

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.445
Was there first beginning to things?"

00:02:41.445 --> 00:02:43.174
ZHUS TSE

00:02:43.174 --> 00:02:45.470
Past tense ZHUS to make a request

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:47.203
or to ask.

00:02:47.203 --> 00:02:48.044
ZHUS TSE

00:02:48.044 --> 00:02:51.630
Now, I think it's fascinating

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.120
that they didn't say YOD.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.330
Okay, Tim for a hundred bucks.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:59.446
Yes, a hundred bucks.

00:02:59.446 --> 00:03:02.496
Why didn't they say to Buddha,

00:03:02.496 --> 00:03:04.970
"Is there a first beginning to things?"

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:09.970
They said, "Is the first
beginning to things evident?"

00:03:14.118 --> 00:03:14.951
MNGON

00:03:14.951 --> 00:03:17.095
Like Abhidharma.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.713
It means "visible" or "manifest."

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.900
They didn't ask Buddha,
"Is there a first beginning

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.930
to the universe or to our
career in the universe?"

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.550
"Is there a beginning

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.737
which is MNGON, which is evident?"

00:03:42.487 --> 00:03:43.900
[student mumbles]

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.170
Yes, good, hundred dollars for Rebecca.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:48.910
Okay.
[student mumbling]

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:51.220
Hundred bucks for Rebecca, yes.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.500
It means, Can you see? You see?

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.160
They didn't say, "Is there a beginning?"

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.110
They said, "Can you close your
eyes and just check for us?"

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.550
Is it MNGON?

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.623
It's very sexy to me that
Nagarjuna chose this verb.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.913
Can you see a beginning?

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.963
Which means "is there?"

00:04:11.963 --> 00:04:12.988
But they didn't say, "Is there?"

00:04:12.988 --> 00:04:15.253
They said, "Can you see a beginning?"

00:04:16.143 --> 00:04:16.976
ZHUS TSE

00:04:16.976 --> 00:04:20.130
THUB PA CHEN POS - The Great Able One,

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.292
meaning the Buddha, Mahamuni.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.259
MIN - nope. Nope.

00:04:26.259 --> 00:04:30.650
"No. I can't see any
beginning to all this mess.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.870
It's beginningless."

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.630
,'KHOR BA THOG MA MTHA' MED DE,

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:40.554
There's no first beginning
of sansara circle.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:45.260
And there's no... MTHA' MED

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:46.850
means "there's no end" either.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.108
Now that's something we got to talk about.

00:04:49.108 --> 00:04:52.570
DE LA... And then they said, "Why not?"

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.120
And he said, "Well,
nothing has a front end

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.683
and a back end and a middle."

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.250
Don't ask me if your career in sansara

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.060
had a beginning and the past,

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.660
had a first beginning and a final end.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:06.493
Why?

00:05:06.493 --> 00:05:07.870
Because nothing has a front end

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:12.418
and nothing has a back end
and nothing has a middle.

00:05:12.418 --> 00:05:13.785
DE LA SNGON...

00:05:13.785 --> 00:05:14.950
But he doesn't say it that way.

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:18.510
He says, ,GANG LA THOG MED MTHA' MED PAR,

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:22.157
anything that doesn't have
a front end and a back end,

00:05:22.157 --> 00:05:26.120
DBUS NI GA LA YOD

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.033
how could you have a middle?

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:31.770
If the place you're going,

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:36.160
if the road you're going
to travel is infinite,

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.593
if the road you have traveled is infinite,

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:41.960
I don't know, where is the middle?

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.550
Where are you going to put the middle?

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:46.530
He's like, "Come on, where's the middle?"

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.080
I like this picture of a ribbon.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.080
Somehow it suggests to me the difficulty

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.977
of defining a beginning.

00:05:59.303 --> 00:06:01.267
I don't know, I just saw
this picture and I thought,

00:06:01.267 --> 00:06:02.867
"Oh wow, that's the one I want."

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:07.073
There's no middle place
on an endless line.

00:06:09.508 --> 00:06:11.163
Let's go to the next verse.

00:06:12.077 --> 00:06:16.003
[coughs] Sorry. [coughs] Sorry.

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:20.319
,DE PHYIR DE LA SNGA PHYI DANG,

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:25.319
,LHAN CIG RIM PA MI 'THAD DO,

00:06:25.404 --> 00:06:29.400
That SNGA PHYI means "earlier and later."

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.110
But Choney Lama explains that Nagarjuna

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:35.840
has now gone on to your lifetimes.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.800
Your lifetimes.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.800
There's no earlier lifetime.
And there's no later lifetime.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.420
There's also no place...

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:47.000
LHAN CIG means where the two meet.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:48.930
Here it means where the two meet.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.414
There's no place where all your past lives

00:06:51.414 --> 00:06:54.400
meets all your future lives.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.000
There's no place where you are living now.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.250
There's no place you're going
to live in your future lives.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:04.530
There's no place you have
lived in your past lives.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.290
And there's no meeting point between

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:09.650
your past lives and your future lives.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.350
So don't ask me about a beginning.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:17.243
By the way, did he really say, no?

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.422
Do you remember?

00:07:22.422 --> 00:07:26.280
LUNG MA BSTAN BCU BZHI

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.280
14, LUNG MA BSTAN 14 unanswered questions.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.713
I believe this is one
of them, or two of them.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:39.146
He refused to answer.

00:07:39.146 --> 00:07:40.854
And then later as his disciples said,

00:07:40.854 --> 00:07:42.680
"Why you refuse to answer the question?"

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.921
He said, "Any answer I give

00:07:44.921 --> 00:07:46.162
they're going to misunderstand it.

00:07:46.162 --> 00:07:47.900
It's better to keep my mouth shut."

00:07:48.919 --> 00:07:51.585
And that applies to a
talking to the press,

00:07:51.585 --> 00:07:53.584
and stuff like that.

00:07:53.584 --> 00:07:58.251
Anything you say is going to be twisted.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:07:59.983
Just no, thank you.

00:08:01.383 --> 00:08:02.616
I found this other picture,

00:08:02.616 --> 00:08:05.374
Okay, Pachi,

00:08:05.374 --> 00:08:08.524
of a guy with a rope.

00:08:09.682 --> 00:08:11.220
Yes.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.820
It means the beginning
is almost undefinable

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:16.640
and the end is undefinable.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:19.640
That's okay. No, it's worse than that.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.530
The middle is also undefinable.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.937
You can't find present moment.

00:08:25.262 --> 00:08:26.237
It's bad enough

00:08:26.237 --> 00:08:29.987
that your lifetimes have no beginning.

00:08:29.987 --> 00:08:30.850
And it's bad enough

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:33.408
that your lifetimes have no end.

00:08:33.408 --> 00:08:34.274
But by the way,

00:08:34.274 --> 00:08:36.290
there's no place where
you are between those two.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.783
That's why the rope is unraveling. Get it?

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:46.353
All right, we're going to go on to...

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.260
It's kind of a denial of
past lives and future lives.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:52.710
Technically, it's called that.

00:08:54.339 --> 00:08:57.130
,GAL TE SKYE BA SNGA GYUR LA,

00:08:59.447 --> 00:09:02.132
,RGA SHI PHYI MA YIN NA NI,

00:09:02.132 --> 00:09:07.094
So which came first: your
birth, or your aging and death?

00:09:08.302 --> 00:09:11.150
In one put it together.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:14.780
Which came first: your birth,
or your aging and death?

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:16.636
Which came first?

00:09:16.636 --> 00:09:18.070
GAL TE SKYE BA SNGA GYUR LA

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:19.786
If your birth came first,

00:09:19.786 --> 00:09:23.055
RGA SHI PHYI MA YIN NA NI

00:09:23.055 --> 00:09:26.046
and then your aging and death came later,

00:09:26.046 --> 00:09:27.921
SKYE BA RGA SHI MED PA DANG

00:09:27.921 --> 00:09:29.846
then you should be able

00:09:29.846 --> 00:09:32.440
to have a birth with no aging and death.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.233
Because in the early part,
it has to stand by itself.

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:42.953
If you can have a birth with
no getting old and dying,

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:48.430
Sorry. If birth came before
getting old and dying,

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:50.840
then birth can be self-standing.

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:54.472
And it doesn't have to depend
on getting old and dying.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:10:00.580
If you say, "My rebirth
into this world came first,

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.390
then I got old in this world and died."

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.680
He says, "No, okay if
that's the way it happened,

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.837
"then SKYE BA RGA SHI MED PA DANG

00:10:08.837 --> 00:10:11.345
"then you can have a rebirth

00:10:11.345 --> 00:10:14.770
"that doesn't have getting old and dying."

00:10:15.625 --> 00:10:18.050
MA SHI BAR YANG SKYE BAR 'GYUR

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:22.218
And you could be born without dying.

00:10:22.218 --> 00:10:24.580
MA SHI BAR

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:27.270
Without dying, you could be born.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:31.170
If being born comes before
getting old and dying,

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:33.540
then you could have a being born

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:34.880
without an aging and dying,

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:39.463
and you could live without
getting old and dying.

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:44.509
Are they interdependent?

00:10:44.509 --> 00:10:45.575
Does one depend on the other?

00:10:45.575 --> 00:10:47.792
That what is the question.

00:10:47.792 --> 00:10:49.060
GAL TE SKYE BA 'PHYI 'GYUR LA

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:53.423
Then the guy says, the guy
is like, a perfect fall guy.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:56.613
He always gives the right wrong answer.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:03.260
And he says, "Oh, oh, GAL
TE SKYE BA 'PHYI 'GYUR LA"

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:07.588
The birth comes after aging and death.

00:11:07.588 --> 00:11:11.630
and RGA SHI SNGA BA YIN NA NI

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.710
Aging and death come first.

00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:16.060
And then you die and you get reborn.

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:19.310
So rebirth is not first.

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.318
Getting old and dying comes first.

00:11:21.318 --> 00:11:22.968
Then you have a rebirth.

00:11:22.968 --> 00:11:24.476
,SKYE BA MED PA'I RGA SHI NI,

00:11:24.476 --> 00:11:25.918
,RGYU MED PAR NI JI LTAR 'GYUR,

00:11:25.918 --> 00:11:26.759
Okay, good.

00:11:26.759 --> 00:11:29.980
So, in the beginning of that process,

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:32.393
when there's just getting old and dying,

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.400
that doesn't depend on anything.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:38.500
You can get old and
die without being born.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.320
- Why? - Being born comes later.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.300
Okay, so you can get old and
die without being born, right?

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.650
- Why? - Well, you said
the being born comes later.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.120
He can't change his story.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.380
He can't say being born comes
before getting old and dying

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.960
because he said being born comes after

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.380
getting gold and dying. Got it?

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:00.790
He started out with
beginning born comes first

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:03.840
and getting old and dying comes later.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:04.870
But then there was a problem

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:08.330
that you could get born and never die.

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.930
Because it would be by itself
and then you'd have it.

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:15.456
And we have a birth here.

00:12:15.456 --> 00:12:19.500
I was looking for a baby to be born.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:22.221
Oh, there it is, my grandson,

00:12:22.221 --> 00:12:24.810
Maitri.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:27.740
A birth with a birth would have no death.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:31.023
A birth that was just a
birth would have no death.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:33.810
He came out like that, by the way.

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:38.083
He was already, looks
like Rodin's "Thinker".

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.250
Did you ever see that, Pachi, in Paris?

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:43.560
Rodin, his estate?

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.530
That's awesome. That guy is up there,,,

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:49.030
That's Maitri.

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:52.683
Next verse.

00:12:54.747 --> 00:12:56.921
,SKYE BA DANG NI RGA SHI DAG,

00:12:56.921 --> 00:12:58.779
,LHAN CIG RUNG BA MA YIN TE,

00:12:58.779 --> 00:13:00.463
,SKYE BZHIN PA NA 'CHI 'GYUR ZHING,

00:13:00.463 --> 00:13:02.163
,GNYIS KA RGYU MED CAN DU 'GYUR,

00:13:02.163 --> 00:13:03.529
Then the guy again,

00:13:03.529 --> 00:13:06.045
He's trying to get out of the problems.

00:13:06.045 --> 00:13:07.440
And he says, "You know what?

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.840
"You're born, and get old
and die at the same time.

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.430
"Don't say one came before the other.

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:15.650
"Because when you say
one came before the other

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:17.920
"there's all these problems."

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.530
Then the one that came before the other

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:21.810
would have to not depend on the other one.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:25.510
So I'll tell you what:
both at the same time.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:27.869
You're born and you die at the same time.

00:13:27.869 --> 00:13:29.219
Then Nagarjuna says,

00:13:29.219 --> 00:13:30.993
LHAN CIG RUNG BA MA YIN TE

00:13:30.993 --> 00:13:32.435
That's not right.

00:13:32.435 --> 00:13:34.727
SKYE BZHIN PA NA 'CHI 'GYUR ZHING

00:13:34.727 --> 00:13:36.620
Then a baby coming out of the womb

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:39.802
would be dying at the
moment. Not possible.

00:13:39.802 --> 00:13:41.580
GNYIS KA RGYU MED CAN DU 'GYUR

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:46.290
And since neither came first,

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:48.197
they would both be causeless.

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:55.650
There would be no born
before getting old and dying.

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.670
And there'd be no getting old and dying

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.200
that caused you to be reborn.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:01.650
Because they were
happening at the same time.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:04.397
Therefore, both would be causeless.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.510
By the way, anytime something is causeless

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:08.890
there's a problem. Why?

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:11.680
It could pop up anywhere all the time.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:13.777
Oh, look, there's death.
Oh, look, there's aging.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:16.330
It'd be happening,

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:19.580
boop, boop, boop, boop, all around you.

00:14:22.790 --> 00:14:26.123
Can being born and dying happen
at the same time? Picture...

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:33.480
Yes. I don't know.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.930
Let's skip to the next one.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:37.097
Veronica's looking over my shoulder,

00:14:37.097 --> 00:14:40.080
"What are you doing with
that picture?" Nevermind.

00:14:40.968 --> 00:14:45.968
She gave up a long time
ago trying to figure out

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:49.503
why I spend all day
finding weird pictures.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:54.205
Here we go. Next one.

00:14:54.205 --> 00:14:58.413
,GANG LA SNGA PHYI LHAN CIG GI,

00:14:58.413 --> 00:15:00.738
,RIM PA DE DAG MI SRID PA'I,

00:15:00.738 --> 00:15:02.562
,SKYE BA RE DANG RGA SHI DE,

00:15:02.562 --> 00:15:04.245
,CI YI PHYIR NA SPRO BAR BYED,

00:15:04.245 --> 00:15:07.963
This is very, very,
very interesting to me.

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:14.126
Look, so birth can't come
before getting old and dying.

00:15:14.126 --> 00:15:15.000
Right?

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:15.833
Right.

00:15:15.833 --> 00:15:20.016
And getting old and dying
can't come before being born.

00:15:20.016 --> 00:15:20.849
Right.

00:15:20.849 --> 00:15:23.850
And we said they can't happen
at the same time, right?

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:24.683
Right.

00:15:24.683 --> 00:15:25.974
So they're MI SRID PA'I, right?

00:15:25.974 --> 00:15:27.920
What's MI SRID PA'I, Tim, do you remember?

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:29.817
So they're impossible. Right?

00:15:29.817 --> 00:15:32.010
And the guy says, "Yes,
they're impossible."

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.740
Then Nagarjuna says, "Well,
why the hell you keep

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:38.161
talking about being born,
and dying and getting old?"

00:15:38.161 --> 00:15:40.486
[Chuckles] CI'I PHYIR

00:15:40.486 --> 00:15:42.783
Why you make up this crap?

00:15:42.783 --> 00:15:44.608
SPRO BAR means...

00:15:44.608 --> 00:15:46.307
Now we got to talk about SPROS PA.

00:15:46.307 --> 00:15:49.458
SPRO BAR means why you
make up all this stuff?

00:15:49.458 --> 00:15:52.624
Why you keep coming up with weird stuff?

00:15:52.624 --> 00:15:55.501
- Oh, first I was born, then
I got old and dead.    - No.

00:15:55.501 --> 00:15:58.980
- Oh, I got old and dead, and
then I got born again.   - No.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:02.136
- Oh, they happened at the
same time.   - No, no, no.

00:16:02.136 --> 00:16:06.580
[chuckles] Then why you keep
thinking of this weird stuff?

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:09.050
So "weird stuff, thinking of weird stuff,

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.062
There's a special word.

00:16:11.062 --> 00:16:11.895
SPROS PA

00:16:11.895 --> 00:16:13.086
And I wanted to talk about it.

00:16:13.086 --> 00:16:15.260
Because to me it's very interesting.

00:16:15.260 --> 00:16:19.410
What's the Sanskrit for
thinking up weird stuff?

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.180
What's the verb? Here we go.

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:24.467
We're going to go to the Sanskrit again,

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:28.020
which is prapancha.

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:29.370
Prapancha. And it's weird.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:31.885
And you're not going to
be comfortable with it.

00:16:31.885 --> 00:16:32.718
[student mutters]

00:16:32.718 --> 00:16:34.563
No, it's hard to find.

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:38.300
And there's a lot of things it could be.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:40.840
So pra- means "forward",

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:45.790
like the prow of a boat, or
a proud person, or forward.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:48.200
So the pra- part is easy.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:49.887
The hard part is √pañc.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:54.330
It can have several meanings.

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:55.805
What's pancha?

00:16:56.896 --> 00:16:58.432
Yes, fire.

00:16:58.432 --> 00:17:00.621
√pañc means fire.

00:17:00.621 --> 00:17:03.400
Pancha means fire.

00:17:03.400 --> 00:17:06.180
Then it came into German's "fünf."

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:08.067
√Pañc changes to "fünf."

00:17:08.067 --> 00:17:10.210
And then it came into English as fire.

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:11.984
What is it in Spanish?

00:17:11.984 --> 00:17:14.730
- Fuego.
- Oh, then forget that.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:18.210
That's a weird thing, which probably

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:19.810
means it was %kavanch before.

00:17:19.810 --> 00:17:21.442
But that's a long story.

00:17:21.442 --> 00:17:23.780
So prapancha.

00:17:23.780 --> 00:17:25.455
I take it to be like this.

00:17:25.455 --> 00:17:29.772
Look...[shows hand movement]

00:17:29.772 --> 00:17:32.360
And the many comes from the one.

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:34.653
So why you keep making up stuff?

00:17:36.799 --> 00:17:41.683
Why you keep making up stuff
when there's nothing there?

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:43.753
Why you SPROS PA?

00:17:43.753 --> 00:17:46.590
SPROS PA means that [shows
hand movement] also.

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:48.850
Or √pac...

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:49.683
Do you know √pac?

00:17:49.683 --> 00:17:51.910
Like Pachi. [chuckles]

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:53.483
The verb in Sanskrit,

00:17:53.483 --> 00:17:56.403
√pac, means "to cook", to cook something.

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:59.780
It used to be pekw- (Indo-European root).

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:02.430
And then that came into
kitchen and cucina.

00:18:04.237 --> 00:18:08.070
And vipaka,

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.230
means when your karma is cooked.

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:14.257
That means karmic ripening.

00:18:14.257 --> 00:18:17.140
And the Sanskrit word for karmic ripening

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:18.973
is super cook, vipaka, vipac.

00:18:23.963 --> 00:18:27.980
That cooking can also have the feeling

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:30.860
of setting the dishes out on the table,

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:34.210
like setting a meal up for a person.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:37.950
So again, it's this idea
of... [shows hand movement]

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:41.323
Why did you make up this feast?

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:46.840
Why you keep making up
birth, aging, death?

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:48.780
Why you keep telling me this?

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:51.370
Why you keep making up this stuff?

00:18:51.370 --> 00:18:53.953
Like presenting a meal.

00:18:53.953 --> 00:18:56.303
[student mumbles] Yes, that too.

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:00.453
There's also another
meaning to the Sanskrit,

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:02.577
which is √pek.

00:19:02.577 --> 00:19:07.577
And √pek in Indo-European
means to be pretty,

00:19:08.285 --> 00:19:09.390
to be beautiful.

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:12.973
And it comes from a verb for combing sheep

00:19:15.544 --> 00:19:18.240
and combing your hair and
making yourself beautiful.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:20.231
So it's called √pek.

00:19:20.231 --> 00:19:25.047
√pek also means to pick
out hair from a sheep

00:19:25.047 --> 00:19:27.200
and to make a sheep beautiful.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:31.190
And that √pekt came into the word "fight."

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:33.350
That's why there's a GH and fight,

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:37.710
which means pulling
somebody's hair. [chuckles]

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:41.050
And there's a kind of
shell called "pectin,"

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:44.620
which looks like a fan.

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:48.507
So again, you get this idea
of... [shows hand movement]

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:52.900
creating reality from a single point,

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:54.233
in many different versions.

00:19:54.233 --> 00:19:57.398
Why are we discussing this verb?

00:19:57.398 --> 00:19:58.850
- [Student] Thinking of weird things?

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:02.250
- Yes. Nagarjuna says,
"Come on, I'm tired of it.

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:07.250
Quit pecking, quit picking
on, pecking on me."

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:09.570
Like, leave it alone, all right?

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:12.210
Why you keep making up
these different things?

00:20:12.210 --> 00:20:14.158
- So what if sansara have a beginning,

00:20:14.158 --> 00:20:16.450
then sansara will have
an end, that sansara...

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:19.717
- Just stop making up
crap. Ok? It doesn't.

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:25.088
This is all your... [shows hand movement].

00:20:25.088 --> 00:20:25.921
√pac.

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:32.930
Stop the Sanskrit lesson.

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:37.190
The difference between making
something out of nothing

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:39.100
and making something out of something.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:40.970
And that's a magician here.

00:20:43.809 --> 00:20:48.255
And I chose this picture
because there is a

00:20:48.255 --> 00:20:50.430
SPROS PA which is legal.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:55.210
Nagarjuna's accusing the guy

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:58.293
of making up false things:

00:20:58.293 --> 00:20:59.759
Beginning to things,

00:20:59.759 --> 00:21:02.050
end to things, middle?

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:04.820
Stop making up stuff.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:06.827
But then if you're Nagarjuna, you say,

00:21:06.827 --> 00:21:11.827
"But by the way, you do always
make up stuff." [Laughs]

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:15.580
Yes, but at least if you
know what you're doing,

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:19.690
you're not making it up that bad. [Laughs]

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:21.463
So yes, we are creating reality.

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:25.870
There's a verb in Tibetan, RIS SU CHAD PA

00:21:25.870 --> 00:21:28.500
which means "to separate hairs",

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:30.516
which means to define reality.

00:21:30.516 --> 00:21:32.383
And that you are doing.

00:21:32.383 --> 00:21:37.383
You are peka, Pachi, you
are creating reality.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:40.740
You are drawing lines and
all... This is where Tim starts.

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:42.733
This is where Tim's computer starts.

00:21:42.733 --> 00:21:44.630
You are making those lines.

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:47.490
But that's not what I'm talking about.

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:50.780
I'm talking about your human need to say

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:53.920
there was a beginning to your lifetimes

00:21:53.920 --> 00:21:56.680
and then there is a final lifetime.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:00.980
There's not. Your mind goes on infinitely.

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:02.730
And it has no beginning and no end.

00:22:03.623 --> 00:22:06.957
And philosophically, you
cannot find the present moment.

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:11.553
Although it feels like there's one.

00:22:11.553 --> 00:22:15.020
Next verse.

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:17.003
If you're not confused yet

00:22:17.003 --> 00:22:18.627
you haven't been listening.

00:22:21.111 --> 00:22:24.269
,'KHOR BA 'BA' ZHIG SNGON GYI MTHA',

00:22:24.269 --> 00:22:26.094
,YOD MA YIN PAR MA ZAD KYI,

00:22:26.094 --> 00:22:27.726
,RGYU DANG 'BRAS BU NYID DANG NI,

00:22:27.726 --> 00:22:28.559
,MTSAN NYID DANG NI MTSAN GZHI NYID,

00:22:28.559 --> 00:22:30.525
,TSOR DANG TSOR PO NYID DANG NI,

00:22:30.525 --> 00:22:32.408
,DON YOD GANG DAG CI YANG RUNG,

00:22:32.408 --> 00:22:33.575
,DNGOS RNAMS THAMS CAD NYID LA YANG,

00:22:33.575 --> 00:22:36.000
,SNGON GYI MTHA' NI YOD MA YIN,

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:39.960
Look, 'KHOR BA 'BA' ZHIG
means it's not just sansara

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:42.177
that's the problem.

00:22:42.177 --> 00:22:44.200
'BA' ZHIG means "only."

00:22:44.200 --> 00:22:46.333
Last chapter. The Yoga Sutra.

00:22:47.984 --> 00:22:48.817
Fifty dollars.

00:22:50.325 --> 00:22:53.447
[student mumbles]
[GMR Chuckles]

00:22:53.447 --> 00:22:55.488
It's called 'BA' ZHIG PA

00:22:55.488 --> 00:22:56.591
TThe one and only.

00:22:56.591 --> 00:23:00.088
Kaivailya.

00:23:00.088 --> 00:23:02.020
The oldest yoga center in India

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:03.365
is called Kaivalya.

00:23:10.299 --> 00:23:15.299
The Center for the One and
Only Beginning of All Things

00:23:15.565 --> 00:23:18.440
or something like that. Kaivalya.

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:23.300
And it became a synonym for
emptiness in the Yoga Sutra.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:25.650
The one and only, 'BA' ZHIG

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:27.430
What's it doing here, Geshe-La?

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:29.544
Stop getting distracted.

00:23:29.544 --> 00:23:31.768
'KHOR BA 'BA' ZHIG means...

00:23:31.768 --> 00:23:34.130
Look, it's not just
sansara that doesn't have

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:36.561
a beginning and an end.

00:23:36.561 --> 00:23:39.351
MA ZAD means it's not only that.

00:23:39.351 --> 00:23:41.159
RGYU DANG 'BRAS BU NYID DANG NI

00:23:41.159 --> 00:23:43.560
You cannot point to a first cause.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:46.371
You cannot point to a final result.

00:23:46.371 --> 00:23:48.396
MTSAN NYID DANG NI MTSAN GZHI NYID

00:23:48.396 --> 00:23:53.379
You cannot point to a first
mental picture of something.

00:23:53.379 --> 00:23:55.050
And you can't point to the first thing

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:56.640
that matched that mental picture.

00:23:56.640 --> 00:23:58.898
Definition and definiendum.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:04.231
TSOR DANG TSOR PO NYID DANG NI

00:24:04.231 --> 00:24:09.231
You cannot point to the first
feeling that was ever felt.

00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:12.620
You cannot point to the first
feeling that was ever felt.

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:15.787
Every feeling felt something.

00:24:15.787 --> 00:24:17.953
And everything that was felt
came from a previous feeling.

00:24:19.928 --> 00:24:23.624
DON YOD GANG DAG CI YANG RUNG

00:24:23.624 --> 00:24:26.257
DON YOD means something which has meaning,

00:24:26.257 --> 00:24:28.423
but it means substantial

00:24:28.423 --> 00:24:30.890
or something concrete.

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:33.216
You can't point to
anything in the universe.

00:24:33.216 --> 00:24:34.207
GANG DAG CI YANG RUNG

00:24:34.207 --> 00:24:35.740
,DNGOS RNAMS THAMS CAD NYID LA YANG,

00:24:35.740 --> 00:24:37.790
,SNGON GYI MTHA' NI YOD MA YIN,

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:41.963
There is no beginning to any
experience in the universe.

00:24:42.847 --> 00:24:44.122
Forget it.

00:24:44.122 --> 00:24:46.120
Nagarjuna's like, "Don't ask me."

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:49.436
Don't ask Buddha "did your
past lives have a beginning?"

00:24:49.436 --> 00:24:50.330
Why?

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:51.580
Because when you hurt your finger

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:53.630
it didn't have beginning either.

00:24:53.630 --> 00:24:55.363
So forget your universe.

00:24:56.490 --> 00:25:00.040
Pick your nose. It didn't
have a beginning either.

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:04.030
He said, "Stop asking me,
stop making up this stuff."

00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:06.488
So this verse about...

00:25:06.488 --> 00:25:09.063
Look, sansara doesn't have any beginning,

00:25:09.063 --> 00:25:11.390
but neither does cause and effect,

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:15.130
neither does definition,
neither does sensation.

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:19.236
Nothing has a beginning. That's Nagarjuna.

00:25:19.236 --> 00:25:21.050
That's when was the first mantra?

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:22.530
When did the mantra begin?

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:27.530
When did the cancer start
to get healed by the mantra?

00:25:27.572 --> 00:25:30.770
And if you don't understand those things,

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:33.690
it's not going to work as well.

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:34.523
All right.

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:38.790
- Oh but it's too difficult
to understand those things.

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:42.358
Or my grandmother will
never understand it,

00:25:42.358 --> 00:25:44.750
Geshe-la, just let her do the mantras.

00:25:44.750 --> 00:25:49.750
- That's okay. If your grandma is 95...

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:52.553
I have a lady I take care of.

00:25:52.553 --> 00:25:54.569
This is our conversation.

00:25:54.569 --> 00:25:56.520
I'll say, "Hi, how you doing?"

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.820
She say, "Mike, how are you?"

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:00.920
I say, "Fine." She say,
"What are you up to?"

00:26:00.920 --> 00:26:03.930
I'll say, "You know,
I've been filming stuff

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.617
with Pachi and Tim."

00:26:05.617 --> 00:26:07.920
And then she'll go, "Oh, that's nice.

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:10.237
"Oh, Mike! What are you up to nowadays?"

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:13.860
And she'll just ask me the same question

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:14.960
over and over again.

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:16.551
And it's interesting,

00:26:16.551 --> 00:26:19.420
me and Veronica read a book about

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:21.930
how to answer older people.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:23.617
And it said, never say,

00:26:23.617 --> 00:26:26.420
you asked me that 10 times today already,

00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:29.840
because they already
feel bad about being old

00:26:29.840 --> 00:26:31.330
and not remembering anything.

00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:36.330
So never get impatient,
just answer nicely.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:41.090
And then that book said,
how you answer nicely

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:44.547
is a sign of your mental health. [Laughs]

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:48.827
You can change and make
it more interesting.

00:26:48.827 --> 00:26:51.249
"Oh, me and Pachi went out for pizza,

00:26:51.249 --> 00:26:52.587
and we had a..."

00:26:52.587 --> 00:26:54.857
And then the third time
she asks you can say

00:26:54.857 --> 00:26:57.010
"Oh, me and Rebecca came from LA..."

00:26:57.010 --> 00:26:58.807
And she's like, "Wow."

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:04.593
So why am I saying that?
I don't remember. [Laughs]

00:27:06.767 --> 00:27:08.510
- [Student] Don't teach
your grandma mantras

00:27:08.510 --> 00:27:11.233
when she's not...
- Yes. Yes. Yes.

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:16.790
If someone is that far gone... Okay.

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:19.330
Just teach them mantra.
Tell them to do the mantra.

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:23.645
Don't try to explain how the mantra works.

00:27:23.645 --> 00:27:27.490
I believe that if a person's over 95,

00:27:27.490 --> 00:27:30.360
don't explain the mantra. In my opinion,

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:31.893
before that, always explain the mantra.

00:27:31.893 --> 00:27:33.780
Because it will work better.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:37.039
So unless their mind is that far gone.

00:27:37.039 --> 00:27:39.972
By the way, even a 95 year old,

00:27:39.972 --> 00:27:42.297
if you try to explain emptiness to them,

00:27:42.297 --> 00:27:44.610
you can get some really sexy results.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:46.833
You can get some amazing results.

00:27:50.447 --> 00:27:52.305
My own mother, my stepmother...

00:27:52.305 --> 00:27:53.638
I was visiting her,

00:27:53.638 --> 00:27:56.910
and I said, "Well,
let's go out for lunch."

00:27:56.910 --> 00:27:59.510
She said, "I can't," and I said "Why?"

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:02.169
And she said, "I got a yoga class."

00:28:02.169 --> 00:28:04.650
And I was like, "What? You're doing yoga?"

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:08.280
And she's 94, right? She's
doing chair yoga or something.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:10.800
I said, "Wow, that's so great, Mom,

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:12.100
I didn't know you were doing yoga."

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:17.100
She said, "I've been doing
yoga for 30 years, every day."

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:20.980
And I'm like, "Oh my
gosh, I didn't know that.

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:22.257
How'd you get started in yoga?"

00:28:22.257 --> 00:28:25.510
"You taught me my first class, you forgot.

00:28:25.510 --> 00:28:27.690
And then I never stopped."

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:32.300
And I just didn't know.
So it can be a surprise.

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:35.140
You can try to explain the deeper meaning

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:37.410
of how mantra works.

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:39.540
The only person you can't explain to is

00:28:39.540 --> 00:28:44.540
if they're totally... a
hundred years old or something.

00:28:44.670 --> 00:28:49.390
But anybody else will
benefit from some explanation

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:51.897
of what's going on with the mantra.

00:28:55.922 --> 00:28:57.964
No beginning, no middle
to all these things.

00:28:57.964 --> 00:29:00.870
And we have an infinity sign.

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:03.857
It's just to make the class
more interesting for you.

00:29:06.862 --> 00:29:09.660
It looks like a car, right? A car.

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:12.650
But don't try to find the starting point.

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:16.720
Don't try to find where you're at now.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:19.063
There isn't any, by itself.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:21.493
All right, let's go ahead.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:23.810
Oh, oh my gosh.

00:29:23.810 --> 00:29:27.970
We finished the class. Is
that possible? [Chuckles]

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:29.710
That was a short one.

00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:33.740
By the way, that's the whole
chapter, I'm sorry. Is it?

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:38.740
Am I crazy? Oh, no I am...
No, I'm not crazy. That's all.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:41.040
That's all. Don't try to find it.

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:44.460
Look. Do your past lives
have beginning? No.

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:47.570
Do your past lives have a final end? No.

00:29:47.570 --> 00:29:49.120
Why are we talking about that?

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:51.120
Because nothing else does either.

00:29:51.120 --> 00:29:53.120
This class doesn't have an end.

00:29:53.120 --> 00:29:55.090
This class doesn't have a beginning.

00:29:55.090 --> 00:30:00.090
This class doesn't have a
halfway point, from its own side.

00:30:01.700 --> 00:30:03.780
If you see this class end suddenly,

00:30:04.869 --> 00:30:07.640
it's because your seeds ran out.

00:30:07.640 --> 00:30:10.090
Nothing has an end of its own.

00:30:10.090 --> 00:30:12.427
Nothing has a beginning of it's own.

